<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/advanced/signal-processing">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    The engineering sequence so far has been about making structures stand and forces
    balance: Statics: Why Structures Stand, Forces, Beams &amp; Bridges and Fluid Mechanics,
    with Units, Estimates &amp; Back-of-Envelope supplying the arithmetic discipline. This
    lesson moves to the invisible half of the subject — information. It extends the
    measurement chain from the intermediate lessons into its digital back half, and feeds
    directly into Control Systems, where filtered signals close feedback loops.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define signals and systems, and explain why one impulse response specifies an LTI system completely;</li>
    <li>use the frequency domain: what the Fourier transform is, and why filtering becomes multiplication there;</li>
    <li>apply the two sampling budgets — Nyquist rate and bit depth — and explain aliasing and quantisation noise;</li>
    <li>plan a filter chain that trades transition width against delay and computation;</li>
    <li>read decibel figures correctly and explain why amplification should follow filtering.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The instrumentation lesson showed how a physical quantity becomes numbers; this lesson is
    what you <em>do</em> with them. A signal — voltage, sound, strain, radio — is variation
    carrying information, buried in other variation that is not. Signal processing is the
    mathematics of separating the two: <strong>Fourier's</strong> insight that any waveform is
    a chord of pure frequencies (so "frequency" becomes a domain you can compute in),
    <strong>Shannon's</strong> sampling law that lets analogue variation become digital
    numbers without losing anything, and the <strong>filtering</strong> craft that keeps the
    band you want and kills the rest. It is also, per the syllabus unit, the back half of the
    measurement chain: amplification, sampling range and rate, A/D conversion, digital
    processing — each stage with its own arithmetic and its own ways to ruin the signal.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="signals, Fourier, sampling, filtering" widget="equationStep" config={sigCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps are the chapter map — read them as one
    measurement chain rather than four topics. Step 2 is the licence for everything that
    follows: if every waveform is a chord, then "which frequencies are present" becomes a
    question you can compute instead of guess. Step 3 carries the caution that recurs all
    lesson: sampling is only safe above twice the highest frequency, and its failure mode —
    aliasing — is silent: data that looks clean and is wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a signal is variation that carries information, and a system is
    anything that transforms it. The next section fixes the vocabulary properly — and shows
    why one short kick can describe an entire system.
  </p>

  <h2>Signals and systems: the vocabulary</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>signal</strong> is a quantity varying over time (or space): the microphone
    voltage from a flute, the strain gauge of a swaying bridge, the pixel columns of a scan
    line. A <strong>system</strong> transforms signals — an amplifier multiplies, a filter
    reshapes, a cable delays and mangles. The most useful class is <strong>linear
    time-invariant (LTI)</strong> systems: scaling the input scales the output, and shifting
    the input shifts the output. LTI systems are tractable because of one fact: they are
    completely described by their <strong>impulse response</strong> — what they do to a single
    infinitely short kick — because any signal can be built from scaled, shifted kicks
    (samples!), and linearity then says the output is the same recipe applied to those
    pieces. Adding a delayed, scaled, summed set of impulse responses is the operation
    called <strong>convolution</strong>, and it is the universal grammar of filtering:
    every filter, analogue or digital, is some impulse response being convolved with your
    signal.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a filter removes noise. It removes noise
    <em>at frequencies different from the signal's</em> — and only that. Noise sitting
    inside the signal's own band is untouchable by any frequency-selective filter,
    which is why the field's cleverer tools exist: averaging (noise averages away while
    the signal adds), lock-in detection (move the signal to an empty band first), and
    model-based estimation. "Filter it" is the beginning of a design conversation, not
    the answer to one.
  </p>

  <h2>Fourier's idea: the frequency domain</h2>
  <p>
    Fourier's claim (1807, greeted with scepticism) is that <em>any</em> reasonable waveform
    is a sum of sine and cosine waves of different frequencies, amplitudes and phases. The
    construction is easiest to believe on the square wave: a sum of odd harmonics —
    sin(f) + sin(3f)/3 + sin(5f)/5 + sin(7f)/7 … — approaches the square shape as terms
    accumulate; even 20 harmonics give a recognisable square with ripples that stubbornly
    overshoot the edges (the <strong>Gibbs phenomenon</strong> — a true square wave needs
    infinite bandwidth, which real systems never have, which is why real "square" pulses
    round off). The <strong>Fourier transform</strong> turns this from a trick into a
    dictionary: every signal has an exact frequency-domain twin — which frequencies, how
    loud, at what phase — and the two representations carry identical information. The
    frequency domain is where signal processing lives because hard problems become easy
    there: filtering is multiplication in frequency (rather than convolution in time);
    noise occupies bands you can see and cut; a vibration spectrum's peaks name its causes
    (a bearing's defect frequency, a structure's resonance). The <strong>discrete Fourier
    transform</strong> is the digital version — N samples in, N complex frequency bins out —
    and the <strong>FFT</strong> computes it in N·log₂N operations instead of N²: for a
    million samples, roughly 2 × 10⁷ operations instead of 10¹², a speed-up of ~50,000×
    that converted Fourier analysis from a batch calculation into something that runs
    inside every phone call, every Wi-Fi packet, every hearing aid.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> every signal has a frequency-domain twin, and hard operations in
    time become easy ones in frequency — which is also why the FFT's speed-up mattered so
    much. But a computer cannot hold a continuous waveform; the next section covers the two
    budgets that govern the crossing to digital.
  </p>

  <h2>Sampling: the bridge to digital, with its two budgets</h2>
  <p>
    Sampling measures the signal at intervals T, at rate f_s. <strong>Nyquist–Shannon's
    theorem</strong>: if the signal contains no frequency above f_s/2, the samples determine
    it <em>exactly</em> — no information is lost, in principle. Sample slower and
    <strong>aliasing</strong> strikes: a 5 kHz tone sampled at 6 kHz is indistinguishable
    from a 1 kHz tone (|5 − 6| = 1), and no software can afterwards tell the impostor from
    the truth — the wagon wheels that rotate backwards in films are this theorem acting on
    your retina. Hence the anti-aliasing filter before the ADC (the control lesson's chain),
    and hence CD audio's 44.1 kHz: hearing ends near 20 kHz, so 44.1 kHz leaves margin for
    a filter to roll off. The second budget is <strong>amplitude</strong>: the ADC assigns
    each sample one of 2ⁿ levels, and the rounding is <strong>quantisation noise</strong>.
    The arithmetic: signal-to-noise ≈ 6.02·n + 1.76 dB, so 16-bit audio carries ~98 dB of
    dynamic range (roughly whisper-to-jackhammer) — each bit bought 6 dB, which is why
    resolution is the first specification anyone checks. Two budgets, two choices —
    sample <em>rate</em> and sample <em>range</em> — and every real acquisition system
    balances them against memory and cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> higher sample rates and bit depths always mean
    better quality. The sampling theorem is about <em>bandwidth</em>, not virtue:
    44.1 kHz already captures the entire audible band, and doubling the rate records
    ultrasound nobody hears while quadrupling the data. Rate and resolution are
    budgets to be spent where the application needs them — a vibration sensor wants
    wide bandwidth at 24-bit depth; a telephone line gets by with 8 kHz and 8 bits —
    and "more" spent anywhere else is storage and cost with no information gained.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two budgets — rate and resolution — decide how faithfully the
    digital samples represent the analogue world. Once the signal is safely in numbers, the
    craft of keeping the band you want begins.
  </p>

  <h2>Filtering: the frequency-selective craft</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>filter</strong> passes some frequencies and attenuates others. The analogue
    workhorse is the RC low-pass: output follows input only sluggishly, so fast fluctuations
    shrink; the cutoff is f_c = 1/(2πRC) — with R = 1.6 kΩ and C = 100 nF, f_c ≈ 1 kHz —
    and above cutoff the response falls 20 dB per decade (tenfold quieter per tenfold
    higher frequency). First-order filters are gentle; steeper skirts come from cascading
    stages (Butterworth for flattest passband, Chebyshev for steeper rolloff with ripple)
    at the price of <strong>phase distortion</strong> — the filter delays different
    frequencies differently, which matters enormously in audio and control (a filter is
    also a delay, and delay in a feedback loop is instability fuel). Digital filters come
    in two families: <strong>FIR</strong> (finite impulse response — a weighted moving
    average; always stable, can be exactly linear-phase) and <strong>IIR</strong>
    (recursive, mimicking the analogue designs, cheaper per tap). The design conversation
    is always the same trade: <strong>transition width versus delay versus
    computation</strong> — you can cut the noise out sharply, or quickly, or cheaply, but
    never all three. And one filter deserves its fame: the <strong>moving average</strong>
    (averaging the last k samples) is a crude low-pass anyone can compute mentally, and
    averaging N independent measurements improves the noise floor as √N — the statistics
    ladder's standard error, running in hardware.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example shows the trade in miniature: the <strong>ECG</strong>. The
    heart's electrical signal lives below about 40 Hz, but the electrode picking it up
    also collects baseline drift (near DC, from skin contact and breathing) and mains
    interference (50 or 60 Hz, radiated from every power line in the room). The
    clinical chain is therefore: a high-pass near 0.5 Hz to kill the drift, a low-pass
    near 40 Hz to kill muscle noise, and a narrow <strong>notch</strong> filter tuned
    to the mains frequency — with the catch that the notch sits close to the signal's
    upper band, so too sharp a notch distorts the very waveform the cardiologist needs
    to read. Every filter in the chain bought cleanliness at the price of some
    fidelity, and the art is spending as little as possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> every filter buys cleanliness at the price of fidelity, and the
    ECG chain showed that trade in miniature. Before you design one, you need a way to score
    the contest between signal and noise — which is exactly what the decibel scale is for.
  </p>

  <h2>Noise, SNR and the decibel</h2>
  <p>
    Noise — thermal (Johnson) noise in every resistor, shot noise in every current,
    interference from everything that radiates — sets the floor under every measurement.
    The <strong>decibel</strong> scale (ratio in powers of ten: 20·log₁₀ of an amplitude
    ratio) exists because signals and noises span eight orders of magnitude; 0 dB is the
    reference, −60 dB is a thousandfold down. Amplification must respect a rule beginners
    learn expensively: <strong>amplify the signal, not the noise</strong> — gain applied
    after filtering is worth ten times gain applied before, because the amplifier adds its
    own noise to whatever it receives. The extreme instrument is the <strong>lock-in
    amplifier</strong>: modulate the measurement at a known reference frequency, then
    demodulate — only the component that follows the reference survives, and signals a
    million times below the broadband noise become measurable (this is how faint optical
    absorptions and nanovolt thermoelectric signals get read). The general version of the
    trick — knowing <em>where</em> in frequency your signal lives — is the whole subject
    compressed to one sentence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the decibel is a unit, like volts. It is a
    <em>ratio</em> on a logarithmic scale — meaningless until a reference is stated,
    which is why the suffixes matter: dBm is against one milliwatt, dBFS against a
    converter's full scale, dBA against a human ear's sensitivity curve. A gain of
    "+20 dB" is a tenfold amplitude ratio and "+10 dB" roughly threefold — adding dB
    values multiplies signals, the arithmetic that makes the scale useful and,
    unlabelled, ambiguous.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it runs the world</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every digital device:</strong> audio, images, radio, Wi-Fi (OFDM = thousands of parallel Fourier channels), radar, medical imaging (the CT scan is Fourier mathematics through a body — the medicine lesson's imaging, computed), seismology (the earth lesson's wave reading).</li>
    <li><strong>Compression is signal processing:</strong> JPEG throws away frequencies the eye weighs less (discrete cosine transform); MP3 throws away sounds the ear masks. A billion photos a day exist affordably because of coefficient budgeting.</li>
    <li><strong>Noise is the enemy, statistics the ally:</strong> lock-in amplifiers pull signals from noise a million times larger; averaging trades time for certainty (the statistics ladder's SE shrinking as √n, in hardware form).</li>
    <li><strong>The digital revolution's law:</strong> Shannon's capacity theorem (bits per second ≤ bandwidth × log(1 + SNR)) sets the ceiling every generation of modems has climbed toward for 75 years — theory leading engineering by decades, exactly as Maxwell led wireless.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the Nyquist–Shannon sampling condition, and name the artefact that appears when it is violated.</li>
    <li>An RC low-pass uses R = 1.6 kΩ and C = 100 nF. Estimate its cutoff frequency, and say roughly what happens to a 100 kHz interference component compared with a 100 Hz signal.</li>
    <li>Why must the anti-aliasing filter act before the ADC rather than after — why can software not repair a badly sampled signal later?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Sample at more than twice the highest frequency present and
    the samples determine the signal exactly. Violate it and <em>aliasing</em> strikes: high
    frequencies masquerade as lower ones, as with the 5 kHz tone sampled at 6 kHz that becomes
    an impostor at 1 kHz. (2) The cutoff is 1/(2πRC), about 1 kHz. The 100 Hz signal passes
    nearly untouched; the 100 kHz component sits two decades above cutoff and falls 20 dB per
    decade, so roughly 40 dB — a hundredfold attenuation. (3) Because aliasing destroys
    information at the instant of sampling rather than adding a removable distortion: once two
    different tones produce identical samples, the difference no longer exists anywhere in the
    data, so no later processing can recover which was true. The filter must remove out-of-band
    content before it can masquerade.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A signal is variation carrying information; an LTI system is fully described by its impulse response, and filtering is convolution.</li>
    <li>Every waveform has a frequency-domain twin, where convolution becomes multiplication and noise becomes visible bands.</li>
    <li>Sampling has two budgets: rate (above twice the highest frequency) and resolution (about 6 dB of dynamic range per bit).</li>
    <li>Aliasing is silent and irreversible — the anti-aliasing filter must act before the ADC, never after.</li>
    <li>Filter design trades transition width against delay against computation; noise inside the signal's own band is untouchable by frequency-selective filters.</li>
    <li>Decibels are logarithmic ratios that need a stated reference; amplify the signal, not the noise — gain after filtering beats gain before.</li>
    <li>Shannon's capacity theorem sets the speed limit that every communication system, from modem to Wi-Fi, climbs toward.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Control Systems is the direct continuation: a filter is also a delay, and this lesson
    flagged delay inside a feedback loop as instability fuel — the control course turns that
    warning into design rules. Materials Selection completes the advanced engineering set on
    the physical side: choosing the components whose behaviour produces the signals this
    lesson processes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The convolution theorem.</strong> The deep structural fact of the field:
    <em>convolution in time is multiplication in frequency</em>. Filtering, smoothing,
    deblurring, echo — all convolutions — become point-by-point multiplications once
    transformed, which is why the FFT is everywhere and why "spectral methods" is almost a
    synonym for signal processing. It also explains artefacts: truncating data (a finite
    window) is multiplying by a rectangle, which <em>convolves</em> the spectrum with the
    rectangle's sinc-shaped spectrum — spectral leakage, the reason every spectrum analysis
    offers window functions (Hann, Hamming) whose gentler edges trade resolution for
    cleanliness.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Information theory keeps score.</strong> Shannon's 1948–49 work gives sampling
    its footnote and channels their speed limit: capacity C = B·log₂(1 + SNR) — a 3 kHz
    telephone line at 30 dB SNR carries ~30 kbit/s, and every modem generation from 300
    baud to 56k was engineering climbing toward exactly that ceiling. Together with source
    coding (compress to the entropy limit) it defines the two ends of every communication
    link, and both ends are signal processing all the way down: OFDM (Wi-Fi, 5G) is the
    FFT applied to pack parallel channels into a band; turbo and LDPC codes are the
    surprising discovery that random-looking codes actually reach Shannon's limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> <strong>Compressed sensing</strong> (2004–) overturned
    a piece of the dogma: if a signal is <em>sparse</em> in some domain (most coefficients
    near zero — images, spectra), it can be reconstructed from far fewer samples than
    Nyquist demands, by solving for the sparsest explanation — used now in MRI
    acceleration and radar. Adaptive filters (LMS, Kalman) track signals whose statistics
    move; wavelets extended Fourier's fixed resolution to multi-resolution analysis
    (JPEG2000, denoising); and neural networks now perform end-to-end DSP tasks — speech
    enhancement, channel equalisation — where hand-designed filters once reigned, raising
    the field's live epistemological question: when the filter is learned, what is its
    guarantee? Classical DSP's answers were theorems; the learned versions so far offer
    benchmarks.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fourier, <em>Théorie analytique de la chaleur</em> (1807/1822); Nyquist (1928); Shannon, "A Mathematical Theory of Communication" (1948) and "Communication in the Presence of Noise" (1949); Cooley &amp; Tukey (1965); Smith, <em>The Scientist and Engineer's Guide to Digital Signal Processing</em> (free online); Candès &amp; Wakin on compressed sensing (2008). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: sampling, filtering and noise" widget="quiz" config={sigQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> treat these four questions as diagnostics for the two
    budgets and the filtering trade. The first is the aliasing trap in action — work out what
    the samples <em>cannot</em> distinguish before reading the explanation. The second and
    third exercise the quantitative backbone above, the 6 dB-per-bit rule and the RC cutoff;
    the fourth explains why the FFT, not the textbook transform, actually runs on hardware.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
